# Cloudflare Pages redirect map.
#
# Every URL the previous WordPress site published, pointed at its replacement.
# These matter more than they look: the old pages have whatever search equity
# the business has built since 2019, and a 301 carries it across where a 404
# throws it away.
#
# Paths that are unchanged between the two sites — /about-us, /packages,
# /contact, /privacy-policy — need no rule and are listed here only so the next
# person can see that they were considered rather than missed.

# EVERY RULE IS LISTED TWICE, WITH AND WITHOUT A TRAILING SLASH. WordPress
# published its pages with the slash (/key-information/), and that is the form
# Google indexed: the Search Console export of 2026-09-08 lists /key-information/,
# /about-us-mobile/, /packages-mobile/ and /contact-mobile/, all slashed. Pages
# matches these rules exactly, so the slash-less rules alone returned 404 for
# every one of those URLs. The earlier "verified 301" was tested without the
# slash, which is not how the old URLs look. Probed on nlds.pages.dev 2026-09-12.
# Unchanged pages (/about-us/, /packages/, /contact/, /privacy-policy/) need no
# rule: Pages itself 308s the slashed form to the page.

/home                       /                 301
/home/                      /                 301
/key-information            /free-resources   301
/key-information/           /free-resources   301
/frequently-asked-questions /faq              301
/frequently-asked-questions/ /faq             301

# The old site 301-redirected its homepage to /?scfm-mobile=1, courtesy of the
# "Specific Content for Mobile" plugin. That plugin does not exist here, so the
# query string is simply ignored and / serves the homepage directly.

# The "Specific Content for Mobile" plugin published a second, parallel copy of
# four pages at a -mobile URL. All four return 200 on the live site and Google has
# indexed at least three of them: /about-us-mobile/ (66 impressions), /packages-mobile/
# (30) and /contact-mobile/ (4) all appear in the Search Console export of
# 2026-09-08. Without these rules they become 404s the day DNS moves, and the
# search equity sitting on them is thrown away rather than folded into the real page.
# Confirmed by request 2026-09-09 — /faq-mobile/, /key-information-mobile/ and
# /privacy-policy-mobile/ do not exist, so they are not listed.
/about-us-mobile            /about-us         301
/about-us-mobile/           /about-us         301
/packages-mobile            /packages         301
/packages-mobile/           /packages         301
/contact-mobile             /contact          301
/contact-mobile/            /contact          301
/home-mobile                /                 301
/home-mobile/               /                 301

# Common WordPress paths that no longer exist, sent somewhere useful rather
# than to a dead end.
/wp-content/*               /                 301
/feed                       /                 301
/feed/                      /                 301
/index.php                  /                 301

# --- Next.js static-export prefetch payloads -----------------------------
#
# There were rewrite rules here. They never worked, for two reasons found by
# probing the deployed site:
#
#   1. Cloudflare Pages `_redirects` supports 301, 302, 303, 307 and 308 only.
#      A status-200 rewrite is silently ignored, so the rules were inert.
#   2. The target did not serve either. `output: "export"` writes the payload to
#      out/<route>/__next.<route>/__PAGE__.txt, and Pages returns 404 for files
#      inside a directory named `__next.<route>` — though files with dots sitting
#      directly in the route directory serve fine.
#
# Fixed in scripts/flatten-rsc-payloads.mjs instead, which runs as `postbuild`
# and copies each payload to the flat name next/link actually requests. No
# redirect needed: the file now exists where it is asked for.
